# Shared Caddy edge snippets — the blocks that are BYTE-IDENTICAL across all three
# edge profiles (docker/Caddyfile, Caddyfile.https, Caddyfile.prod). Extracted here
# so an edge change (e.g. the T2-09 forward_auth pass on /datasets/*) lands ONCE
# instead of three times (T2-76). Each profile `import`s these named snippets; the
# profile-specific parts (site address, TLS, the built-bundle vs vite catch-all)
# stay inline in each Caddyfile.
#
# Caddy resolves `import` relative to the importing Caddyfile's directory, so this
# file must be mounted alongside each Caddyfile at /etc/caddy/ (a Caddyfile import
# cannot see an unmounted file). The caddy service in docker-compose.yml mounts it
# read-only; the https/prod overrides inherit that mount (they only replace the
# Caddyfile mount itself, by container target).

# Static versioned dataset assets. handle_path strips the /datasets URL prefix,
# and the root points at the on-disk datasets/ subtree (decision D-30 — dataset
# trees live at {DATA_ROOT}/datasets/, disjoint from the upload jails under
# {DATA_ROOT}/users/), so /datasets/{ds_id}/tiles/grid/grid_v1.pmtiles ->
# {DATA_ROOT}/datasets/{ds_id}/tiles/... The PUBLIC URL shape is unchanged; only
# the filesystem root moved. All paths here embed the dataset_version, making
# immutable caching correct. The renderer HTTP-Range-reads {z}/{x}/{y} tiles out
# of each .pmtiles container; file_server answers Range with 206 Partial Content.
#
# T2-09 / decision D-A: the static edge is GATED by a per-request forward_auth
# sub-request to the API's stateless authz endpoint BEFORE file_server serves any
# byte. On a 2xx the fetch proceeds and the bytes stay on file_server (no proxy
# hop — the D-A performance premise); on a 401/403 the API's response is returned
# and file_server is never reached. The check is a µs-cheap JWT verify (no DB), so
# the per-tile byte-range fan-out is cheap even at 1M. Landing it here means all
# three edge profiles gain the gate in ONE edit (the T2-76 dedupe payoff).
#
# handle_path has already STRIPPED the /datasets prefix by the time forward_auth
# runs, so its default X-Forwarded-Uri would carry only /{ds_id}/... — we override
# it with {http.request.orig_uri} so the endpoint receives the ORIGINAL
# /datasets/{ds_id}/... path and can extract the id unambiguously (verified: the
# stripped form drops the /datasets segment). The browser's HttpOnly, path-scoped
# `viz_ds` cookie rides the same-origin fetch and forward_auth copies it upstream.
#
# ONLY VIEWER ASSETS ARE SERVED (T2-publishing-a-dataset-publishes-its-build-log).
# A dataset directory also holds files that nobody chose to publish: ingest.log (the
# username the build ran as, source paths, bake statistics), progress.json, and any
# *.bak an operator leaves at its root. Before this rule, publishing a dataset
# published all of them. The rule is an ALLOW-list, so it fails closed for a build
# artifact that does not exist yet. It works by prefix: a file left INSIDE tiles/,
# positions/, tags/ or detail/ is still served, e.g. the unreferenced tags sidecar
# that a roles edit leaves on disk (worker.py).
#
# The list is what the viewer fetches, not what the directory holds. Every static
# fetch in the frontend goes through assetUrl() in api-client/client.ts, and its
# callers compose only manifest paths under tiles/, positions/, tags/ and detail/.
# The manifest, cover, presentation and metadata come through /api, which reads
# them from disk, so they are not on the list. Public metadata stays readable
# through GET /api/datasets/{ds}/metadata, by design.
# tests/contract/test_edge_allowlist.py reads the list from THIS file and requires
# every static path in every v2 fixture manifest to pass it. If the pipeline emits a
# new kind of static asset, add its prefix here or the viewer gets a 404.
#
# The 404 is decided BEFORE the forward_auth gate. So an off-list path answers the
# same for every caller, with or without a credential, for a dataset that exists or
# not. It tells an outsider nothing and costs no sub-request. `invoke` is ordered
# before `handle_path` by Caddy's fixed directive order, not by where it sits in this
# file. The gate block below is unchanged: an allowed path behaves exactly as before.
#
# The path matchers clean the path before they match, as file_server does before it
# serves. So `tiles/../ingest.log` (and its %2e%2e spelling) is judged as
# `ingest.log` and answers 404. A `..` that resolves to an ALLOWED path still reaches
# the gate, which refuses any `..` with a 403 (routers/authz.py). The step
# `authz.traversal` in tests/e2e/run_acceptance.py depends on that 403.
#
# A CHANGE HERE NEEDS THE CADDY CONTAINER RECREATED. Every profile sets `admin off`
# and runs without --watch, so Caddy reads this file only when it starts. It is a
# single-file bind mount, and `git pull` replaces the file with a new inode, which a
# running container does not see. `docker compose ... up -d` does not recreate caddy
# either, because its compose configuration did not change. Run
# `docker compose -f <profile> up -d --force-recreate caddy`.
&(dataset_file_not_served) {
	respond 404
}

(datasets_edge) {
	@not_a_viewer_asset {
		path /datasets/*
		not path_regexp ^/datasets/[^/]+/(tiles|positions|tags|detail)/
	}
	invoke @not_a_viewer_asset dataset_file_not_served
	handle_path /datasets/* {
		forward_auth api:8000 {
			uri /api/authz/datasets
			# Forward the ORIGINAL request URI (pre-strip) so the endpoint sees
			# /datasets/{ds_id}/... and can reconstruct the dataset id.
			header_up X-Forwarded-Uri {http.request.orig_uri}
		}
		root * {$DATA_ROOT:/data}/datasets
		header Cache-Control "public, max-age=31536000, immutable"
		file_server
	}
}

# API surface (incl. the manifest endpoint) -> uvicorn. Keep /api prefix.
(api_edge) {
	handle /api/* {
		reverse_proxy api:8000
	}
}

# Shared security-header snippet (R-S5 / RELEASE_READINESS §2). Defined here for
# reuse but IMPORTED ONLY by the public edge (docker/Caddyfile.public), so it does
# NOT change the dev / https / prod profiles (they never import it). HSTS lives in
# this snippet because its only importer is an HTTPS-only site block — sending
# Strict-Transport-Security over plain HTTP would be ignored by browsers anyway,
# but scoping it to the public edge keeps the intent explicit. `preload` is
# intentionally omitted until the domain is submitted to the HSTS preload list;
# raise max-age only after verifying the cert renews cleanly.
(security_headers) {
	header {
		# Force HTTPS for one year, including subdomains (HTTPS-only block).
		Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains"
		# Refuse browser MIME-sniffing of responses.
		X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff"
		# Forbid framing (clickjacking) — legacy header plus the modern CSP below.
		X-Frame-Options "DENY"
		# Minimal CSP: restrict framing only (does NOT constrain script/style/img,
		# so it cannot break the Three.js app); a fuller policy is a users-gate item.
		Content-Security-Policy "frame-ancestors 'none'"
		# Trim the Referer on cross-origin navigations.
		Referrer-Policy "strict-origin-when-cross-origin"
		# Drop the server banner (less fingerprinting).
		-Server
	}
}
